    id: '1',
    title: 'The Brave Umbrella',
    content:
      "After Sunday service, the sky looked like it had tipped over a giant bucket.\n\nThe church doors opened, and *whoosh!* a cold wind chased everyone outside. Rain tapped on cars, danced on puddles, and splashed on shoes. The children—Tomi and Ada—held Mum’s hands and stared at the storm.\n\n“Uh-oh,” Tomi whispered. “We forgot our raincoats.”\n\nMum checked her bag. No raincoats. No spare hat. Just one small umbrella—the kind that looks brave until it meets a real Manchester rain.\n\nAda looked up at Mum. “So… who gets it?”\n\nMum opened the umbrella with a *pop!* The inside smelled a little like mint gum and old sunshine. Then Mum crouched down and pulled both children close.\n\n“You two,” she said, gently guiding them under the umbrella. “All the way in.”\n\n“But Mum,” Tomi said, seeing raindrops land on Mum’s shoulders, “you’re getting wet!”\n\nMum’s hair began to curl, and her jacket darkened from the rain. Still, she held the umbrella steady, like a captain steering a tiny ship through a stormy sea.\n\nAda frowned. “Why do you always do that?”\n\nMum smiled, even as a raindrop slid down her nose. “Because parents are like umbrellas. Sometimes we take the splash so you can stay warm and dry.”\n\nThey started walking to the car park, stepping around puddles like they were hopping across little lakes. Every few steps, Mum tilted the umbrella so the children stayed covered.\n\nA man rushing by dropped his papers. The wind tried to steal them. Mum quickly lowered the umbrella to shield the papers from the rain while Tomi and Ada helped pick them up.\n\n“Thank you!” the man said, holding his papers tightly.\n\nMum nodded. “We all need a bit of cover sometimes.”\n\nWhen they finally reached the car, the children were warm and mostly dry. Mum’s jacket was damp, but her eyes were bright.\n\nInside the car, Ada leaned into Mum’s side. “Mum… you’re a really brave umbrella.”\n\nMum laughed softly. “And you two are my precious passengers.”\n\nAs they drove home, the rain kept tapping the windows like a bedtime drum. Tomi sighed happily. “Next time,” he said, “I’ll carry an umbrella too—so I can be brave for someone else.”\n\nMum smiled at him in the mirror. “That,” she said, “is how kindness grows.”",
