    id: '2',
    title: 'The Sharing Game',
    content:
      "David had the shiniest new toy car in the whole family—silver paint, black wheels, and a tiny spoiler that made it look like it could zoom to the moon.\n\nHe loved it so much that he kept it close, like a secret treasure. He even parked it in a special spot on his bedside table and whispered, “Goodnight, Speedy,” before sleeping.\n\nOne Saturday afternoon, David’s cousin, Kola, came to visit. Kola’s eyes widened the moment he saw the toy car.\n\n“Wow…” Kola breathed. “That’s amazing.”\n\nDavid’s tummy did a funny flip. He didn’t say anything. He just pulled the car closer.\n\nThey sat on the living-room rug. Mum put on soft music. The house smelled like jollof rice warming in the kitchen.\n\nKola tried to play with some blocks, but his gaze kept drifting back to the car. He didn’t ask. He didn’t complain. He just looked… a little sad.\n\nDavid pretended not to notice, but his heart felt tight, like a shoelace tied too hard.\n\nThen David remembered Sunday school. The teacher had talked about how Jesus welcomed people—how He shared His time, His attention, and His love.\n\nDavid stared at Speedy. “It’s mine,” he whispered.\n\nAnd then a quieter voice inside him answered, “Yes… but love grows when it’s shared.”\n\nDavid took a deep breath—the kind that fills your chest like a balloon. He picked up the car and walked over to Kola.\n\nKola looked up, surprised.\n\nDavid placed Speedy gently into Kola’s hands.\n\n“Do you want to race?” David asked.\n\nKola’s face lit up like a lamp. “Really?!”\n\n“Really,” David said, still feeling a little nervous.\n\nThey made a track with pillows, books, and a cereal box tunnel. They counted down together.\n\n“Three… two… one… GO!”\n\nSpeedy zoomed under the tunnel. Kola laughed so loudly the dog barked in excitement.\n\nDavid noticed something strange: he wasn’t losing anything. In fact, the game felt bigger now—like the room had more sunshine.\n\nLater, when the rice was ready, Mum called, “Dinner time!”\n\nKola carefully parked Speedy beside David. “Thanks,” he said quietly. “That was the best part of my day.”\n\nDavid felt warm inside, like he’d swallowed a tiny cup of hot chocolate.\n\nThat night, when Kola left, David stood by the door and waved.\n\nAs the car drove away, David looked back at Speedy on the table.\n\nHe whispered, “Goodnight, Speedy.”\n\nThen he added, smiling, “And goodnight, Sharing Game. Let’s play again soon.”",
