    id: '3',
    title: 'The Little Star That Could',
    content:
      "High above the rooftops, where the air is crisp and quiet, lived a tiny star named Twinkle.\n\nTwinkle was not the biggest star. He didn’t sparkle like the grand stars who loved to show off. And he wasn’t bright like the stars that made people point and say, “Ooooh!”\n\nTwinkle was small.\n\n“Too small,” he often sighed, watching the bigger stars shimmer proudly.\n\nOne night, the clouds arrived—big, puffy, gray ones that covered the moon like a blanket.\n\nThe sky became darker than usual. Down below, a puppy named Biscuit wriggled out of his yard after chasing a leaf. The leaf rolled, and the puppy followed, and soon Biscuit was far from home.\n\nHe sniffed the air. He turned left. He turned right.\n\nEverything looked the same.\n\nBiscuit whimpered.\n\nTwinkle saw him.\n\nHe tried to shine, but his light felt tiny, like a candle in a giant room.\n\n“The big stars will help,” Twinkle told himself.\n\nBut the big stars were scattered far across the sky, and the thick clouds swallowed much of their light.\n\nBiscuit took a step, then another, and nearly bumped into a low fence.\n\nTwinkle’s star-heart thumped.\n\n“I’m too small,” he whispered… and then he remembered something his star-grandmother once told him:\n\n“Even a small light can be a guide if it shines on purpose.”\n\nTwinkle squeezed his eyes shut. He took the biggest breath a star can take. He thought about warm homes, safe hugs, and the puppy’s worried face.\n\nThen—*fwoooom!*—Twinkle pushed his light outward.\n\nNot enough to light the whole world.\n\nBut enough.\n\nA soft beam slipped between the clouds and landed on the path near Biscuit’s paws.\n\nBiscuit blinked.\n\nHe followed the glow like it was a gentle finger pointing the way: one step… two steps… around the fence… back toward familiar smells.\n\nTwinkle held the light steady, even as the clouds wiggled and the wind swirled.\n\nFinally, Biscuit saw his own gate.\n\nHe barked once—happy and loud—and bounded home.\n\nTwinkle felt something inside him stretch wide, like a smile.\n\nWhen the clouds drifted away, the moon reappeared and the big stars sparkled again.\n\nA bright star nearby winked at Twinkle. “Nice work, little one.”\n\nTwinkle glowed, shy but proud.\n\nHe learned that night that being small doesn’t mean being useless.\n\nSometimes, the world doesn’t need the biggest light.\n\nSometimes, it just needs *your* light—right when it matters most.",
