    id: '6',
    title: "The Moon's Night Light",
    content:
      "One evening, the children finished dinner, washed their hands, and padded into their pyjamas. The house smelled like soap and warm blankets.\n\nMum dimmed the lights. Dad read a bedtime story. Everyone yawned.\n\nBut outside… the Sun refused to go to bed.\n\nHe hovered at the edge of the sky, glowing like a giant golden lamp.\n\n“I’m just finishing a chat!” the Sun said to the clouds. “We were talking about shapes—did you know some clouds look like dinosaurs?”\n\nThe clouds giggled and stretched into a long dragon shape.\n\nThe Moon waited behind the horizon, tapping her silvery foot.\n\n“It’s my turn,” the Moon whispered.\n\nBut the Sun kept chatting.\n\nDown below, the children’s eyes stayed wide.\n\n“It’s too bright,” Tomi complained. “My brain thinks it’s lunchtime!”\n\nAda pointed at the window. “The birds are confused. Look—one is trying to sing!”\n\nThe world felt upside down.\n\nThe Moon sighed. She floated up, gentle and calm. Her glow was soft like a night cuddle.\n\nShe drifted closer to the Sun.\n\n“Excuse me,” the Moon said kindly.\n\nThe Sun didn’t hear.\n\nThe Moon cleared her throat. “Ahem.”\n\nStill nothing.\n\nSo the Moon reached out and *tap tap* tapped the Sun on the shoulder.\n\nThe Sun blinked. “Oh! Moon! Hello! Did you see that cloud-dinosaur? Its tail is amazing!”\n\nThe Moon smiled, but her eyes were serious. “Sun… the children need rest. Their bodies grow when they sleep. Their dreams are waiting. The night is part of the world too.”\n\nThe Sun frowned. “But I like talking. And shining. And—”\n\n“And you do it wonderfully,” the Moon said. “But even bright things need to rest. Come. Let the sky breathe.”\n\nThe Sun looked down. He saw parents pulling curtains, kids squinting, babies fussing.\n\n“Oh,” the Sun whispered. “I didn’t mean to keep them awake.”\n\nThe Moon nodded. “Sometimes we don’t notice when we’re taking up too much space. That’s why we help each other.”\n\nThe Sun yawned—a huge, glowing yawn that made the clouds wobble.\n\n“Alright,” he said. “I’ll go.”\n\nSlowly, the Sun dipped below the horizon.\n\nAs he left, he whispered to the Moon, “Thank you for reminding me.”\n\nThe Moon rose higher and turned on her special night light.\n\nNot a harsh light.\n\nA gentle one.\n\nIt painted rooftops silver.\nIt made puddles sparkle.\nIt gave the world permission to be quiet.\n\nIn the children’s rooms, eyelids grew heavy.\n\nMum smiled. “Look,” she whispered, “the Moon is watching over you.”\n\nAda snuggled in. “Goodnight, Moon.”\n\nTomi sighed. “Thanks for the night light.”\n\nOutside, the Moon shimmered softly.\n\nAnd the whole world drifted into sweet, safe dreams—exactly on time.",
