    id: '7',
    title: "Sarah's Magic Red Shoes",
    content:
      "Sarah’s red shoes were the brightest shoes on her street.\n\nThey were not just red—they were *fire-engine red*, the kind of red that looks like it’s smiling.\n\nWhen Sarah ran, she felt like the wind had to chase *her*.\n\n“These are magic shoes,” Sarah told her little brother, Tunde.\n\nTunde squinted. “Do they have spells?”\n\nSarah wiggled her toes. “Yes. The spell is: *Zoom!*”\n\nOne afternoon, Sarah raced to the park. The sky was blue, the grass was springy, and the swings squeaked happily.\n\n“Ready?” Sarah shouted.\n\nTunde nodded.\n\nSarah sprinted.\n\nHer red shoes pounded the ground—tap tap tap—and she flew past the slide.\n\nBut then… her foot caught on a hidden tree root.\n\n*Thump!*\n\nSarah tumbled and landed on her knee.\n\nOuch.\n\nTears rushed to her eyes like rain.\n\n“My shoes didn’t work!” she cried. “My magic is broken!”\n\nDad hurried over and knelt beside her. He gently cleaned her knee with a tissue and gave her a big hug.\n\n“Does it hurt?” he asked.\n\nSarah sniffed. “Yes. And I feel embarrassed. Everyone saw.”\n\nDad squeezed her hand. “Come here,” he said, pointing to her chest. “Do you feel that?”\n\nSarah blinked. “My heart?”\n\n“Yes,” Dad said. “Your brave heart. The magic isn’t in the shoes, Sarah. The magic is in you—your legs that try again, your mind that learns, your heart that doesn’t give up.”\n\nSarah looked down at her red shoes.\n\nThey were still shiny.\n\nStill smiling.\n\nBut maybe Dad was right.\n\nTunde sat beside her. “You can still run,” he said softly. “Even if you’re not magic.”\n\nSarah laughed a tiny laugh through her sniffles. “Maybe I *am* magic,” she whispered. “Just not in the way I thought.”\n\nDad helped her stand.\n\nSarah took one step.\n\nThen another.\n\nHer knee stung, but her courage stood taller.\n\nShe walked back to the starting spot.\n\nTunde’s eyes widened. “You’re going again?”\n\nSarah nodded. “Not because I have magic shoes. Because I have a brave heart.”\n\nShe started slowly this time.\n\nHer feet found the ground.\n\nHer eyes watched for roots.\n\nHer breath moved like a steady drum.\n\nThen she sped up—faster, faster—until she was running with a smart kind of confidence.\n\nWhen she reached the end, she threw her hands up.\n\n“I did it!” she cheered.\n\nDad clapped. Tunde jumped.\n\nSarah looked down at her red shoes and grinned.\n\n“Okay,” she whispered, “maybe you’re not magic.\n\nBut you’re my reminder.\n\nAnd *I* am the one who runs.”",
