    id: '9',
    title: "Andy the Ant's Big Dream",
    content:
      "Andy was an ant so small that a single breadcrumb felt like a giant boulder.\n\nBut Andy had a dream so big it could barely fit inside his tiny ant-head.\n\nHe wanted to reach the very top of the Great Oak Tree.\n\nThe Great Oak was famous in the garden. Its trunk was wide like a castle wall. Its branches stretched like roads in the sky.\n\n“Why would you want to go up there?” asked an ant named Nia.\n\nAndy’s eyes shone. “Because I want to see what the world looks like from above. I want to know what’s possible.”\n\nThe other ants laughed.\n\n“It’s too high!”\n\n“You’ll fall!”\n\n“You’ll get tired!”\n\nAndy didn’t argue. He simply packed a tiny leaf-water bottle and a crumb of biscuit for energy.\n\nThen he started.\n\nThe bark was rough like a mountain.\n\nAndy climbed.\n\nOne step.\n\nTwo steps.\n\nTen steps.\n\nHis legs burned. His arms trembled.\n\nHe rested on a crack in the bark and watched a ladybird march past like a shiny red bus.\n\n“Going somewhere?” the ladybird asked.\n\n“The top,” Andy panted.\n\nThe ladybird whistled. “That’s bold.”\n\nAndy smiled. “I know.”\n\nDay one: Andy reached the first big branch. He slept inside a curled leaf and listened to the wind telling bedtime stories.\n\nDay two: a drizzle made the bark slippery. Andy almost slid, but he grabbed a bump in the wood just in time.\n\nHe whispered, “I can do hard things,” and kept going.\n\nLater, a friendly butterfly fluttered near.\n\n“Need a ride?” the butterfly offered.\n\nAndy looked up at those giant wings.\n\nIt was tempting.\n\nBut Andy shook his head. “Thank you… but I want to do this myself. If I ride, I won’t learn what my legs can do.”\n\nThe butterfly nodded respectfully. “Then I’ll fly beside you, just in case.”\n\nThat made Andy feel less alone.\n\nDay three: the air became cooler and sweeter, filled with leaf smell and sunshine.\n\nAndy climbed past a squirrel’s nest.\n\nThe squirrel peeked out. “Where are you going, tiny traveler?”\n\n“The top!” Andy called.\n\nThe squirrel blinked. “You’re brave. Take this.”\n\nHe tossed down a sunflower seed.\n\nAndy grinned. “Thank you!”\n\nAt last, Andy reached the highest branch.\n\nThe world opened wide.\n\nHe could see the garden paths like ribbons. He could see the rooftops far away. He could even see tiny humans walking like moving dots.\n\nAndy’s ant-heart swelled.\n\nHe whispered, “Wow.”\n\nThe butterfly landed beside him. “Worth it?”\n\nAndy nodded, smiling so hard his cheeks hurt.\n\n“It’s not just the view,” Andy said. “It’s knowing I didn’t quit.”\n\nWhen Andy climbed down and returned home, the ants gathered around.\n\n“Did you really do it?” they asked.\n\nAndy nodded.\n\nNia’s eyes widened. “So… what’s at the top?”\n\nAndy smiled. “A bigger world… and a bigger me.”\n\nAnd from that day, whenever any ant felt too small to try, Andy would say:\n\n“Start.\nClimb.\nRest.\nTry again.\n\nSmall steps can reach big dreams.”",
